Z||000000||84 2in1||06-19-2001||10:13 PM||||Well boys and girls looks like Im gonna try to put more grunt into my Duke by doing a very mild port job then turbochargeing the little one. Only need to figure out how to step up the fuel amount and where to mount the turbo in relation to the throddle body. I have a spare head,intake and exhaust manifolds to work with. Are there any other injectors that will fit in place of the presant one ? Can I pull the injector off a 300 throdle body an put it in place of mine?||||reg||6
Z||000001||84Bill||06-19-2001||10:16 PM||||Here is a link that might help. <A HREF="http://dukemods.50megs.com/WEB%20PAGE/PAGE1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://dukemods.50megs.com/WEB%20PAGE/PAGE1.html</A> <BR>Keep us 2.5ers posted  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||erikred||06-20-2001||05:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84 2in1:<BR><B>Well boys and girls looks like Im gonna try to put more grunt into my Duke by doing a very mild port job then turbochargeing the little one. Only need to figure out how to step up the fuel amount and where to mount the turbo in relation to the throddle body. I have a spare head,intake and exhaust manifolds to work with. Are there any other injectors that will fit in place of the presant one ? Can I pull the injector off a 300 throdle body an put it in place of mine?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Using a blow through set up would probably be the easiest. Mount the turbo to the bottom of the exhaust manifold after welding a new flange to it. abricate some brackets to support the trubo so the manifold is not cracked from the extra stress. Then you should be able to rotate the compressor housing so it is pointing towards the top of the engine. Make a air box for the top of the TBI and plumb tubing into it from the turbo. If you decide to go with a pull through setup make sure you use a turbo with the fuel proof seal.<P>Many injectors should swap directly in to the Duke's TBI, even from Chryslers and fords. Your junck yard will have many to choose from. Ask to look in there core pile and pill one from a bad TBI. Check the impedance first to make sure it is electronicaly compatible. The problem is going to be figuring out which ones flow at what rate. If you find out let me know. You can also increase fuel flow by increasing the fuel pressure. This can be done by installing shims under the regulator spring, or possibly by converting the regulator into an adjustable unit.<P>You may be able to increase the TBI air flow by putting a spacer under the injector and raising it out of the bore some. You may also be able to have the TBI bored out and have larger blades installed. Grinding down the screws and knife edging the blades can aslo help to increase flow.<BR>||||reg||14
